{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfyojrbNpfS8CthLRZGzM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No, stop tuning the thresholds. Your generator already knows where every figure goes, so you can check that directly instead of guessing it back from pixels.\n\n**Why the pixel detectors stay noisy:** they try to recover geometry from the rendered output. Antialiasing, light or white image edges, backgrounds that bleed into the slide, compression artifacts and renderer differences all look like signal. Each new threshold fixes one case and breaks another. That's a sign you're checking at the wrong layer, not that the thresholds need more work.\n\n**Check the geometry where it's created instead:**\n\n1. **Have the generator record placements.** Each time it places a figure, log the slide number, element ID and box (x, y, width, height) in slide units (EMU, points or inches) to a JSON file.\n2. **Assert against that file:**\n   - The box sits inside the slide, or inside your safe-area margins.\n   - No two boxes overlap unless you meant them to.\n   - Each image's aspect ratio matches its box, or you know it will be cropped and that's intended.\n3. **Fail the build when an assertion fails.** Unlike the pixel scripts, these checks are exact and repeatable, with no false positives from rendering noise.\n\n**What geometry alone won't catch, and how to cover it:**\n- **Text that overflows its box.** The box can be in bounds while the text inside spills out. Measure the text with real font metrics (for example, PIL `ImageFont.getbbox` with the same font file), or turn on shrink-on-overflow and check for it.\n- **Cropping inside the figure.** If an image is scaled to fill its box and gets cropped, compare the source image size to the box.\n- **Renderer quirks.** If the final output goes through PowerPoint or LibreOffice and its layout differs from yours, keep one narrow pixel check. Compare the render to a known-good reference image of the same slide, rather than trying to find clipping from scratch. Comparing against a reference is far less noisy.\n\nSo use exact assertions on the placement data as your main check. Pixel analysis is only worth keeping as a narrow fallback for what the placement data can't show you.\n\nIf you point me at the generator, I can add the placement logging and write the assertion script."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11556,"cache_read_input_tokens":27257,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11556},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ff01f16c-7b3f-5ea5-8051-7a95ac7cd1d1","uuid":"7791d884-642f-43c0-9596-12b4e412361a","timestamp":"2026-10-13T07:01:49.847Z","request_id":"req_011CfyojrPTsdcGphYsJtQDM"}
